  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 220 PM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA closed mid- level low will continue moving through the Great \nBasin and into the SW CONUS tonight into Wednesday. The CWA will \ngenerally be unaffected by this system aside from some spotty \nrain/snow showers along the MT/WY/ID borders through this evening.\nOtherwise, light winds and lingering surface moisture will favor \nsome patchy fog redevelopment later tonight into Wednesday \nmorning. The southwest wind protected valleys and the Milk River \nValley will see the highest concentration of fog.\n\nA trough and attendant frontal system looks to dive southeastward\nthrough the plains Wednesday into Thursday. This shouldn't exert \nmuch of a change to the sensible weather other than some gusty \npre- frontal winds over the plains followed by a brief northerly \nwind shift. \n\nRidging aloft then develops during the second half of the \nworkweek, which will bring a return to above average temperatures \nheading into the weekend. The ridge will be broken down by a broad\ntrough that quickly passes through the Northern Rockies and \nbrings gusty to localized strong winds to the Rocky Mountain Front\nand the plains Saturday into Sunday. \n\nNearly all ensemble suites highlight troughing settling over the \nwestern CONUS for much of next week; however, the individual \nmembers indicate the troughs will be undergoing shearing, which \ndecreases forecast positioning and timing confidence. Overall, \nthere's a general expectation of cooling temperatures and \nincreased instances of rain and snow next week, especially for \nmountain areas. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nPatchy dense fog again tonight...\n\nLight winds and lingering surface moisture will encourage patchy \ndense fog to develop again tonight into Wednesday morning. The \nlatest HREF probabilities for visibility falling to less than a \nhalf mile are now running above 50% from Gallatin valley northward\nto Helena and then over the Milk River Valley. Confidence for \nimpactful visibility reductions is highest in Bozeman/Belgrade and\nnearby areas given today's fog stubbornly lingering into the \nafternoon hours. A dense fog advisory has been issued for the \nGallatin Valley and the neighboring Canyon Ferry and Missouri \nHeadwaters zones. Will hold off on fog products for the Helena \narea and the Milk River Valley for now, but these areas will \ncontinue to be monitored heading into this evening. - RCG",
